//? Title: Conformal Prediction: Coverage Without Assumptions
//? Subtitle: Split conformal prediction sets and intervals, their exact finite-sample coverage law, adaptive scores for heteroscedastic regression and class-conditional sets, checked by hand and by simulation and applied to the Palmer penguins, using datamunge's stats::SplitConformalClassifier and stats::SplitConformalRegressor
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
How can any model's predictions be given error bars that are guaranteed to hold, without
assuming the model is right? Split conformal prediction calibrates a set or interval on held-out
data. It covers a new case with probability at least \(1-\alpha\) whenever the calibration and new data
are exchangeable. This report derives the guarantee and the exact coverage law. The coverage is
\(k/(n+1)\) on average, and the coverage a single calibration set delivers is Beta\((k,n+1-k)\)
distributed. Simulations reproduce both to within simulation error. With noise that grows with \(x\),
a linear model's classical interval and the absolute-residual conformal interval both cover 100%
where the noise is small and about 70% where it is large. Conformalized quantile regression covers
90–92% in every region and stays at 91% under a covariate shift. On the Palmer penguins, conformal
sets around a naive Bayes classifier cover 90.5% overall, as predicted. They cover only 77% of
Chinstraps, and class-conditional calibration raises that to 92%. The library gained normalised
and quantile-band regression scores, class-conditional and non-empty sets, conformal p-values and
the coverage law.
)

\toc

> The problem

A fitted model returns a point prediction: a class label, or a number. To act on it we need to
know how far to trust it. Classical prediction intervals, such as those of a linear model, are
exact only if the model is correctly specified: right mean, constant variance, normal errors.
Class probabilities from a classifier are often badly calibrated, and a flexible model such as
a nearest-neighbour rule or a boosted ensemble offers no interval at all. We want a set of
labels, or an interval, that contains the true answer for a new case with a stated probability,
say 90%, for *any* model, without assuming that the model is right or that the data follow a
particular distribution.

Conformal prediction (Vovk, Gammerman and Shafer, 2005) does this. It needs one assumption:
that the data used to calibrate and the new case are *exchangeable*. Any reordering of them is
equally likely; independent draws from one distribution are the usual example. The guarantee
holds in finite samples, not only asymptotically.

> The method

>> Split conformal prediction

Divide the labelled data into a *training* part and a *calibration* part of size \(n\). Fit any
model on the training part. Define a *nonconformity score* \(s(x,y)\) that measures how unusual the
pair \((x,y)\) looks to the fitted model, such as the absolute residual \(\lvert y-\hat\mu(x)\rvert\). Compute it for
every calibration pair, \(s_1,\dots,s_n\), and take

$$
\hat q=s_{(k)},\qquad k=\lceil(n+1)(1-\alpha)\rceil,
$$
\alttext(q hat is the k-th smallest calibration score, where k is the ceiling of n plus 1 times 1 minus alpha.)

the \(k\)-th smallest score (`conformal_quantile()`), or \(\hat q=+\infty\) when \(k>n\). The prediction set for a
new \(x\) is every \(y\) whose score would not exceed the threshold:

$$
C(x)=\{\,y:\ s(x,y)\le\hat q\,\}.
$$
\alttext(C of x is the set of all y whose score s of x and y is at most q hat.)

>> Why it works

The model is fixed once the training part is set aside, so the calibration scores and the score
\(s_{n+1}\) of the new case are exchangeable too. The rank of \(s_{n+1}\) among the \(n+1\) scores is then
uniform on \(\{1,\dots,n+1\}\) (ties broken at random). The new case is covered exactly when \(s_{n+1}\le s_{(k)}\),
that is, when its rank is at most \(k\):

$$
P\big(Y_{n+1}\in C(X_{n+1})\big)\ \ge\ \frac{k}{n+1}\ \ge\ 1-\alpha .
$$
\alttext(The probability that the new response lies in its prediction set is at least k over n plus 1, which is at least 1 minus alpha.)

With continuous scores (no ties) the first inequality is an equality, so the coverage is less
than \(1-\alpha+1/(n+1)\): the method is never far too conservative. The \(+1\) in \(n+1\) is the finite-sample
correction. If \(k>n\), no finite threshold can certify the level, and the set is everything.

>> The coverage a given calibration set delivers

The guarantee averages over the calibration set. For one calibration set, the coverage of
future cases is \(F(\hat q)\), where \(F\) is the distribution function of the score. Since \(F(s_i)\) are \(n\)
independent uniforms, \(F(s_{(k)})\) is their \(k\)-th order statistic:

$$
F(\hat q)\ \sim\ \text{Beta}(k,\;n+1-k),\qquad \operatorname{sd}=\sqrt{\frac{k(n+1-k)}{(n+1)^2(n+2)}} .
$$
\alttext(F of q hat follows a Beta distribution with parameters k and n plus 1 minus k, with standard deviation equal to the square root of k times n plus 1 minus k over n plus 1 squared times n plus 2.)

A calibration set of 20 at \(\alpha=0.1\) delivers coverage that varies from set to set with standard
deviation 0.062. `conformal_coverage(n, alpha)` returns \(k\), \(k/(n+1)\), the Beta parameters and this
standard deviation.

>> P-values

Equivalently, each candidate \(y\) has the conformal p-value
\(p(y)=\big(1+\#\{i:s_i\ge s(x,y)\}\big)/(n+1)\), and \(C(x)=\{y:p(y)>\alpha\}\) (up to ties). The p-value of the true
label is super-uniform: \(P(p(Y)\le t)\le t\). `SplitConformalClassifier::p_values()` returns them.

>> Scores for classification

With fitted class probabilities \(\hat p_1(x),\dots,\hat p_K(x)\):

- **LAC** (least ambiguous set-valued classifier; Sadinle, Lei and Wasserman, 2019):
  \(s(x,y)=1-\hat p_y(x)\). The set is every class with \(\hat p_y(x)\ge1-\hat q\). This gives the smallest
  average set size among sets with the coverage guarantee, when the probabilities are correct.
  But it can return empty sets, and its coverage is uneven: high for easy cases, low for hard
  ones.
- **APS** (adaptive prediction sets; Romano, Sesia and Candès, 2020): \(s(x,y)=\sum_{j:\hat p_j\ge\hat p_y}\hat p_j\), the total
  probability of the classes at least as likely as \(y\). The set is the most likely classes while
  their total probability stays at most \(\hat q\), so it grows where the model is uncertain. With this
  deterministic rule it is empty when the top class alone has probability above \(\hat q\).
- **Non-empty sets** (`nonempty`): add the most probable class to an empty set. The sets only
  grow, so the guarantee holds, with some extra coverage.
- **Class-conditional (Mondrian) calibration** (`class_conditional`) computes one threshold per
  class from that class's calibration rows. The guarantee then holds within every class,
  \(P(Y\in C(X)\mid Y=c)\ge1-\alpha\), which matters when one class is rare or hard. The price is fewer
  calibration points per threshold.

>> Scores for regression

`SplitConformalRegressor` uses \(s=\max(\ell(x)-y,\;y-u(x))/\sigma(x)\) for a base band \([\ell,u]\) and scale \(\sigma>0\),
giving the interval \([\ell(x)-\hat q\sigma(x),\;u(x)+\hat q\sigma(x)]\). Three choices give the standard methods:

- **absolute residual** (Lei et al., 2018): \(\ell=u=\hat\mu\), \(\sigma=1\). The interval \(\hat\mu(x)\pm\hat q\) has the same width
  everywhere.
- **normalised residual** (`normalized()`): \(\ell=u=\hat\mu\) and \(\sigma(x)\) a fitted estimate of the noise
  spread. The width grows where the noise does.
- **conformalized quantile regression** (CQR; Romano, Patterson and Candès, 2019;
  `quantile_band()`): \(\ell,u\) fitted \(\alpha/2\) and \(1-\alpha/2\) quantile regressions, \(\sigma=1\). The fitted band is
  widened (\(\hat q>0\)) or narrowed (\(\hat q<0\)) just enough to make its coverage exact.

All three have the same marginal guarantee. They differ in *conditional* coverage, the coverage
at a particular \(x\), which no method can guarantee without assumptions (Lei and Wasserman,
2014). A good score makes it roughly constant.

> Simple examples

>> Nine calibration points by hand

A regression model's absolute residuals on nine calibration points are 0.3, 1.2, 0.4, 2.5, 0.9,
0.1, 1.7, 0.6 and 0.8. For \(\alpha=0.2\), \(k=\lceil10\times0.8\rceil=8\): the threshold is the 8th smallest residual,
1.7, and every interval is \(\hat\mu(x)\pm1.7\). A new point with residual 1.0 is covered, with p-value
\((1+\#\{s_i\ge1\})/10=(1+3)/10=0.4\). The coverage law is \(k/(n+1)=0.8\) on average, Beta(8, 2) given the
calibration set.

For classification with classes *a*, *b* and *c*, take nine calibration rows with true classes and
probabilities as in the first table below. LAC scores are \(1-\hat p_y\), and APS scores add the
probabilities of the classes at least as likely as the true one.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

using DF = dm::dstruct::DataFrame;

int main() {
  // Regression.
  const std::vector<double> residuals{0.3, 1.2, 0.4, 2.5, 0.9, 0.1, 1.7, 0.6, 0.8};
  const dm::stats::SplitConformalRegressor reg(std::vector<double>(9, 0.0), residuals, {.alpha = 0.2});
  const auto law = reg.coverage();
  std::print("Regression: q = {}, interval for a prediction of 5: [{}, {}], k = {}, mean coverage {}, Beta({}, {})\n\n",
             reg.quantile(), reg.predict_interval(5.0).first, reg.predict_interval(5.0).second, law.rank, law.mean,
             law.beta_a, law.beta_b);

  // Classification.
  const std::vector<std::string> classes{"a", "b", "c"};
  const std::vector<std::vector<double>> probs{{0.7, 0.2, 0.1}, {0.5, 0.4, 0.1}, {0.2, 0.6, 0.2}, {0.1, 0.8, 0.1},
                                               {0.3, 0.3, 0.4}, {0.6, 0.3, 0.1}, {0.2, 0.2, 0.6}, {0.4, 0.5, 0.1},
                                               {0.1, 0.1, 0.8}};
  const std::vector<std::string> truth{"a", "b", "b", "b", "a", "a", "c", "a", "c"};
  const dm::stats::SplitConformalClassifier lac(probs, truth, classes, {.alpha = 0.2, .score = "lac"});
  const dm::stats::SplitConformalClassifier aps(probs, truth, classes, {.alpha = 0.2, .score = "aps"});
  std::vector<double> pa, pb, pc;
  for (const auto& p : probs) pa.push_back(p[0]), pb.push_back(p[1]), pc.push_back(p[2]);
  DF cal;
  cal.add_column("true class", truth);
  cal.add_column("p(a)", pa);
  cal.add_column("p(b)", pb);
  cal.add_column("p(c)", pc);
  cal.add_column("LAC score", lac.calibration_scores());
  cal.add_column("APS score", aps.calibration_scores());
  std::print("{}\n\nThresholds: LAC {}, APS {}\n\n", cal.to_md(2), lac.quantile(), aps.quantile());

  const std::vector<std::vector<double>> queries{{0.85, 0.10, 0.05}, {0.45, 0.45, 0.10}, {0.35, 0.33, 0.32}};
  std::vector<std::string> q_text, lac_set, aps_set, lac_p;
  auto join = [](const std::vector<std::string>& s) {
    std::string out = "{";
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < s.size(); ++i) out += (i ? ", " : "") + s[i];
    return out + "}";
  };
  for (const auto& q : queries) {
    q_text.push_back(std::format("({}, {}, {})", q[0], q[1], q[2]));
    lac_set.push_back(join(lac.predict_set(q))), aps_set.push_back(join(aps.predict_set(q)));
    const auto p = lac.p_values(q);
    lac_p.push_back(std::format("{:.1f}, {:.1f}, {:.1f}", p[0], p[1], p[2]));
  }
  DF sets;
  sets.add_column("probabilities (a, b, c)", q_text);
  sets.add_column("LAC set", lac_set);
  sets.add_column("LAC p-values", lac_p);
  sets.add_column("APS set", aps_set);
  std::print("{}\n", sets.to_md());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Regression: q = 1.7, interval for a prediction of 5: [3.3, 6.7], k = 8, mean coverage 0.8, Beta(8, 2)

| true class | p(a) | p(b) | p(c) | LAC score | APS score |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| a | 0.7 | 0.2 | 0.1 | 0.3 | 0.7 |
| b | 0.5 | 0.4 | 0.1 | 0.6 | 0.9 |
| b | 0.2 | 0.6 | 0.2 | 0.4 | 0.6 |
| b | 0.1 | 0.8 | 0.1 | 0.2 | 0.8 |
| a | 0.3 | 0.3 | 0.4 | 0.7 | 1.0 |
| a | 0.6 | 0.3 | 0.1 | 0.4 | 0.6 |
| c | 0.2 | 0.2 | 0.6 | 0.4 | 0.6 |
| a | 0.4 | 0.5 | 0.1 | 0.6 | 0.9 |
| c | 0.1 | 0.1 | 0.8 | 0.2 | 0.8 |


Thresholds: LAC 0.6, APS 0.9

| probabilities (a, b, c) | LAC set | LAC p-values | APS set |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| (0.85, 0.1, 0.05) | {a} | 1.0, 0.1, 0.1 | {a} |
| (0.45, 0.45, 0.1) | {a, b} | 0.4, 0.4, 0.1 | {a, b} |
| (0.35, 0.33, 0.32) | {} | 0.2, 0.2, 0.2 | {a, b} |
// result_end

The regression threshold is the 8th smallest residual, 1.7, as computed by hand. In the
classification table, the eighth smallest of the nine LAC scores is 0.6, so a class is admitted
when its probability is at least 0.4. The eighth smallest APS score is 0.9, so classes are
admitted in order of probability while their running total stays at or below 0.9.

The three queries show how the two scores behave. A confident prediction (0.85 for *a*) gives
the singleton \(\{a\}\) under both. A tie between *a* and *b* gives \(\{a,b\}\) under both. For a near-uniform
row, no class reaches probability 0.4, so LAC returns the *empty* set. Every class's p-value is
\((1+1)/10=0.2\), not above \(\alpha=0.2\). An empty set is a definite statement that the case looks unlike
every calibration example, and it still counts as a miss for the guarantee. APS returns \(\{a,b\}\)
for this row, because the total 0.68 stays below 0.9.

>> Heteroscedastic regression

Responses \(y=1+0.5x+(0.2+0.3x)\varepsilon\) with \(x\sim U(0,10)\) and \(\varepsilon\sim N(0,1)\): the mean is linear, but the noise
standard deviation grows from 0.2 to 3.2. There are 500 training points, 500 calibration points
and 5000 test points, and \(\alpha=0.1\). Four intervals are compared:

- the linear model's classical 90% prediction interval, which assumes constant variance;
- the absolute-residual conformal interval around the same linear fit;
- the normalised conformal interval, with \(\sigma(x)\) a linear regression of the training residuals'
  absolute values on \(x\);
- CQR with linear quantile regressions for the 5% and 95% quantiles.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cp_bands.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

using DF = dm::dstruct::DataFrame;

DF draw(dm::random::Xoshiro256StarStar& rng, std::size_t n, double lo = 0.0, double hi = 10.0) {
  std::vector<double> x(n), y(n);
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    x[i] = rng.uniform(lo, hi);
    y[i] = 1.0 + 0.5 * x[i] + (0.2 + 0.3 * x[i]) * rng.normal(0.0, 1.0);
  }
  DF d;
  d.add_column("x", x);
  d.add_column("y", y);
  return d;
}

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::random::Xoshiro256StarStar rng(7);
  const DF train = draw(rng, 500), cal = draw(rng, 500), test = draw(rng, 5000);
  const auto y_cal = cal.get_column<double>("y").values(), y_test = test.get_column<double>("y").values();
  const auto x_test = test.get_column<double>("x").values();

  const dm::stats::LM lm(train, "y ~ x");
  // Spread model: absolute training residuals regressed on x.
  DF spread = train;
  const auto fit_train = lm.predict(train);
  std::vector<double> abs_res;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < fit_train.size(); ++i) abs_res.push_back(std::abs(train.double_at("y", i) - fit_train[i]));
  spread.add_column("abs_res", abs_res);
  const dm::stats::LM scale_model(spread, "abs_res ~ x");
  auto scale = [&](const DF& d) {
    auto s = scale_model.predict(d);
    for (auto& v : s) v = std::max(v, 0.05);
    return s;
  };
  const dm::stats::QuantileRegression q05(train, "y ~ x", {.quantile = 0.05}), q95(train, "y ~ x", {.quantile = 0.95});

  const auto mu_cal = lm.predict(cal), mu_test = lm.predict(test);
  const dm::stats::SplitConformalRegressor absolute(mu_cal, y_cal, {.alpha = 0.1});
  const auto normalised = dm::stats::SplitConformalRegressor::normalized(mu_cal, scale(cal), y_cal, {.alpha = 0.1});
  const auto cqr = dm::stats::SplitConformalRegressor::quantile_band(q05.predict(cal), q95.predict(cal), y_cal, {.alpha = 0.1});

  const auto classical = lm.predict(test, dm::stats::PredictionInterval::Prediction, 0.9);
  const std::size_t m = y_test.size();
  dm::stats::SplitConformalRegressor::Evaluation lm_ev;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
    lm_ev.covered.push_back(y_test[i] >= classical.lower[i] && y_test[i] <= classical.upper[i]);
    lm_ev.widths.push_back(classical.upper[i] - classical.lower[i]);
    lm_ev.coverage += lm_ev.covered.back() / double(m), lm_ev.average_width += lm_ev.widths.back() / double(m);
  }
  const auto ones = std::vector<double>(m, 1.0);
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::string, dm::stats::SplitConformalRegressor::Evaluation>> evals{
      {"linear-model interval", lm_ev},
      {"conformal, absolute", absolute.evaluate(mu_test, y_test)},
      {"conformal, normalised", normalised.evaluate(mu_test, mu_test, scale(test), y_test)},
      {"conformal, CQR", cqr.evaluate(q05.predict(test), q95.predict(test), ones, y_test)},
  };
  std::print("Calibrated quantiles: absolute {:.4f}, normalised {:.4f}, CQR {:.4f}\n\n", absolute.quantile(), normalised.quantile(),
             cqr.quantile());

  // Coverage and mean width overall and in five bins of x.
  std::vector<std::string> names;
  std::vector<double> overall, width;
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> bins(5);
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> bin_cov(evals.size(), std::vector<double>(5));
  for (std::size_t e = 0; e < evals.size(); ++e) {
    const auto& [name, ev] = evals[e];
    names.push_back(name), overall.push_back(ev.coverage), width.push_back(ev.average_width);
    std::vector<double> hit(5, 0.0), cnt(5, 0.0);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
      const std::size_t b = std::min<std::size_t>(4, std::size_t(x_test[i] / 2.0));
      hit[b] += ev.covered[i], cnt[b] += 1.0;
    }
    for (std::size_t b = 0; b < 5; ++b) bin_cov[e][b] = hit[b] / cnt[b], bins[b].push_back(hit[b] / cnt[b]);
  }
  DF table;
  table.add_column("interval", names);
  table.add_column("coverage", overall);
  table.add_column("mean width", width);
  for (std::size_t b = 0; b < 5; ++b) table.add_column(std::format("x in [{}, {})", 2 * b, 2 * b + 2), bins[b]);
  DF::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 3;
  std::print("{}\n\n", table.to_md(md));

  // Covariate shift: test points only from x in [8, 10].
  const DF shifted = draw(rng, 5000, 8.0, 10.0);
  const auto ys = shifted.get_column<double>("y").values();
  const auto mus = lm.predict(shifted);
  const auto cls = lm.predict(shifted, dm::stats::PredictionInterval::Prediction, 0.9);
  double lm_cov = 0.0;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < ys.size(); ++i) lm_cov += (ys[i] >= cls.lower[i] && ys[i] <= cls.upper[i]) / double(ys.size());
  const auto ones_s = std::vector<double>(ys.size(), 1.0);
  DF shift;
  shift.add_column("interval", names);
  shift.add_column("coverage when every test x is in [8, 10]",
                   std::vector<double>{lm_cov, absolute.evaluate(mus, ys).coverage,
                                       normalised.evaluate(mus, mus, scale(shifted), ys).coverage,
                                       cqr.evaluate(q05.predict(shifted), q95.predict(shifted), ones_s, ys).coverage});
  std::print("{}\n", shift.to_md(md));

  // Figure: three conformal bands over 400 test points, and coverage by bin.
  const auto blue = dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, grey = dm::RGB{148, 163, 184};
  std::vector<double> grid;
  for (int i = 0; i <= 100; ++i) grid.push_back(0.1 * i);
  DF g;
  g.add_column("x", grid);
  const auto mu_g = lm.predict(g), s_g = scale(g), l_g = q05.predict(g), u_g = q95.predict(g);
  std::vector<double> px(x_test.begin(), x_test.begin() + 400), py(y_test.begin(), y_test.begin() + 400);
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2, 2);
  for (int panel = 0; panel < 3; ++panel) {
    std::vector<double> lo, hi;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < grid.size(); ++i) {
      const auto [a, b] = panel == 0   ? absolute.predict_interval(mu_g[i])
                          : panel == 1 ? normalised.predict_interval(mu_g[i], mu_g[i], s_g[i])
                                       : cqr.predict_interval(l_g[i], u_g[i], 1.0);
      lo.push_back(a), hi.push_back(b);
    }
    std::vector<double> bx = grid, by = hi;
    bx.insert(bx.end(), grid.rbegin(), grid.rend());
    by.insert(by.end(), lo.rbegin(), lo.rend());
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.polygon(bx, by, dm::graphics::tint(blue, 0.8), true).points(px, py, grey, 3.0).lines(grid, mu_g, blue, 1.5);
    p.x_limits(0.0, 10.0).y_limits(-8.0, 16.0).x_label("x").y_label("y")
        .title(panel == 0 ? "Absolute residual" : panel == 1 ? "Normalised residual" : "Conformalized quantile regression");
    fig.add(p);
  }
  auto cov = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const std::vector<double> centres{1, 3, 5, 7, 9};
  const dm::RGB colors[4] = {{17, 24, 39}, {220, 38, 38}, {37, 99, 235}, {22, 163, 74}};
  const char* short_names[4] = {"linear model", "absolute", "normalised", "CQR"};
  for (std::size_t e = 0; e < evals.size(); ++e)
    cov.lines(centres, bin_cov[e], short_names[e], colors[e], 2.0).points(centres, bin_cov[e], colors[e], 6.0);
  cov.lines({0.0, 10.0}, {0.9, 0.9}, grey, 1.0);
  cov.x_limits(0.0, 10.0).y_limits(0.6, 1.08).title("Coverage by x (bins of width 2)").x_label("x").y_label("coverage");
  fig.add(cov).size(1100, 840).fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/cp_bands.svg");
}
```
