| conformal, CQR | 0.906 | 5.609 | 0.900 | 0.900 | 0.909 | 0.916 | 0.904 |


| interval | coverage when every test x is in [8, 10] |
| :--- | ---: |
| linear-model interval | 0.703 |
| conformal, absolute | 0.728 |
| conformal, normalised | 0.931 |
| conformal, CQR | 0.910 |

\image(figures/cp_bands.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Top left, top right and bottom left: 90% conformal bands (pale blue) around the linear fit (blue line) or the fitted quantiles, with 400 of the test points (grey). Bottom right: coverage within five bins of \(x\) for the linear model's classical interval (black), and the absolute (red), normalised (blue) and quantile-regression (green) conformal intervals; the grey line is the 90% target.)
\alttext(Four panels. Three show the same scatter of test points fanning out from a narrow strip near x equals 0 to a wide spread near x equals 10, around a rising blue line. The absolute-residual band has constant width, too wide on the left and too narrow on the right. The normalised and quantile-regression bands widen from left to right with the points. The fourth panel plots coverage in five bins of x: the linear-model and absolute-residual curves fall from 1.0 to about 0.7, the normalised curve rises from 0.82 to about 0.92, and the quantile-regression curve stays flat near 0.9.)

All four intervals cover close to 90% overall: 88.6% for the linear model's interval, 89.8% for
the absolute and normalised conformal intervals, and 90.6% for CQR. The conformal ones have the
guarantee; the classical one is near 90% here only because the over- and under-coverage happen to
balance. The bins show the difference. The linear model's interval assumes a constant noise
variance, and the absolute-residual conformal interval has a constant width too. Both are much
too wide where the noise is small, with 100% coverage for \(x<2\). Both are too narrow where it is
large, covering only 70% and 72% for \(x\ge8\). The marginal guarantee holds by averaging
over-coverage on the left with under-coverage on the right.

The two adaptive scores spread the coverage more evenly. CQR covers 90.0% to 91.6% in every bin
and has the narrowest intervals on average (5.61, against 6.34 for the absolute residual). Its
calibrated correction \(\hat q=0.04\) is small, because the linear quantile regressions are nearly
correct here, and conformal calibration only fine-tunes them. The normalised interval covers
90–93% from \(x=2\) on but only 82% in the first bin. That is the weak point of the method: it is only
as good as its spread model, here a straight line fitted to absolute residuals, floored at 0.05.

*Covariate shift.* When every test point comes from \(x\in[8,10]\), the calibration and test data are
no longer exchangeable, and the guarantee is void. The absolute-residual interval then covers
72.8% and the classical one 70.3%. The adaptive intervals still cover 93.1% (normalised) and
91.0% (CQR). They were not guaranteed to, but their scores are roughly identically distributed
across \(x\), so the shift hardly changes the score distribution.

> Checking the theory

>> The exact marginal coverage

For continuous scores the theory gives coverage exactly \(k/(n+1)\), between \(1-\alpha\) and \(1-\alpha+1/(n+1)\).
Here the scores are absolute standard normal residuals, with a fresh calibration set and test
point in each of 100000 replicates.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  dm::random::Xoshiro256StarStar rng(11);
  const int reps = 100000;
  std::vector<double> ns, ks, theory, empirical, se, upper;
  for (const std::size_t n : {8u, 9u, 10u, 19u, 20u, 49u, 99u, 999u}) {
    const auto law = dm::stats::conformal_coverage(n, 0.1);
    double hits = 0.0;
    std::vector<double> cal(n);
    for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
      for (auto& v : cal) v = std::abs(rng.normal(0.0, 1.0));
      hits += std::abs(rng.normal(0.0, 1.0)) <= dm::stats::conformal_quantile(cal, 0.1);
    }
    ns.push_back(double(n)), ks.push_back(double(law.rank)), theory.push_back(law.mean);
    empirical.push_back(hits / reps), se.push_back(std::sqrt(law.mean * (1.0 - law.mean) / reps));
    upper.push_back(std::min(1.0, 0.9 + 1.0 / (double(n) + 1.0)));
  }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("n", ns);
  table.add_column("k", ks);
  table.add_column("k / (n + 1)", theory);
  table.add_column("simulated coverage", empirical);
  table.add_column("simulation SE", se);
  table.add_column("upper bound 0.9 + 1/(n + 1)", upper);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 4;
  md.column_digits = {{"n", 0}, {"k", 0}};
  std::print("{}\n", table.to_md(md));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| n | k | k / (n + 1) | simulated coverage | simulation SE | upper bound 0.9 + 1/(n + 1) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 8 | 9 | 1.0000 | 1.0000 | 0.0000 | 1.0000 |
| 9 | 9 | 0.9000 | 0.9010 | 0.0009 | 1.0000 |
| 10 | 10 | 0.9091 | 0.9093 | 0.0009 | 0.9909 |
| 19 | 18 | 0.9000 | 0.9003 | 0.0009 | 0.9500 |
| 20 | 19 | 0.9048 | 0.9045 | 0.0009 | 0.9476 |
| 49 | 45 | 0.9000 | 0.9006 | 0.0009 | 0.9200 |
| 99 | 90 | 0.9000 | 0.8998 | 0.0009 | 0.9100 |
| 999 | 900 | 0.9000 | 0.9009 | 0.0009 | 0.9010 |
// result_end

Every simulated coverage is within about one simulation standard error of \(k/(n+1)\). The table
shows the finite-sample correction at work. For \(n=8\), \(k=9>n\): no threshold can certify 90%, the set
is everything, and the coverage is 1. For \(n=9\) the threshold is the largest score, with coverage
exactly 0.9. For \(n=10\), \(k=10\) gives \(10/11=0.909\), and for \(n=20\), \(19/21=0.905\). The excess over 0.9 is
always less than \(1/(n+1)\) and vanishes as \(n\) grows: 0.9009 at \(n=999\).

>> The coverage of one calibration set is Beta distributed

With standard normal errors, a calibration set with threshold \(\hat q\) covers future cases with
probability exactly \(2\Phi(\hat q)-1\). Over 20000 calibration sets of size 20 and of size 200, its
distribution should be Beta\((k,n+1-k)\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cp_beta.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::random::Xoshiro256StarStar rng(13);
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  std::vector<double> ns, mean_sim, mean_th, sd_sim, sd_th, low_sim, low_th;
  for (const std::size_t n : {20u, 200u}) {
    const auto law = dm::stats::conformal_coverage(n, 0.1);
    std::vector<double> coverage;
    std::vector<double> cal(n);
    for (int r = 0; r < 20000; ++r) {
      for (auto& v : cal) v = std::abs(rng.normal(0.0, 1.0));
      coverage.push_back(2.0 * dm::random::normal_cdf(dm::stats::conformal_quantile(cal, 0.1)) - 1.0);
    }
    double m = 0.0, s = 0.0, below = 0.0;
    for (const double c : coverage) m += c / coverage.size(), below += (c < 0.85) / double(coverage.size());
    for (const double c : coverage) s += (c - m) * (c - m) / (coverage.size() - 1.0);
    ns.push_back(double(n)), mean_sim.push_back(m), mean_th.push_back(law.mean), sd_sim.push_back(std::sqrt(s)), sd_th.push_back(law.sd);
    low_sim.push_back(below), low_th.push_back(dm::random::beta_cdf(0.85, law.beta_a, law.beta_b));

    // Histogram (density scale) with the Beta density.
    const double lo = n == 20 ? 0.6 : 0.84, hi = n == 20 ? 1.0 : 0.96;
    const int nb = 40;
    std::vector<double> centres(nb), heights(nb, 0.0);
    for (int b = 0; b < nb; ++b) centres[b] = lo + (b + 0.5) * (hi - lo) / nb;
    for (const double c : coverage) {
      const int b = int((c - lo) / (hi - lo) * nb);
      if (b >= 0 && b < nb) heights[b] += 1.0 / (coverage.size() * (hi - lo) / nb);
    }
    std::vector<double> gx, gy;
    for (int i = 0; i <= 300; ++i) {
      const double x = lo + (hi - lo) * i / 300.0;
      gx.push_back(x), gy.push_back(dm::random::beta_pdf(x, law.beta_a, law.beta_b));
    }
    const double top = 1.15 * std::max(*std::max_element(heights.begin(), heights.end()), *std::max_element(gy.begin(), gy.end()));
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.bars(centres, heights, "simulated", dm::RGB{147, 197, 253}, 0.9 * (hi - lo) / nb);
    p.lines(gx, gy, std::format("Beta({}, {})", law.beta_a, law.beta_b), dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2.0);
    p.segments({0.9}, {0.0}, {0.9}, {0.7 * top}, dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.5);
    p.x_limits(lo, hi).y_limits(0.0, top).title(std::format("n = {} calibration points", n))
        .x_label("coverage of future cases").y_label("density");
    fig.add(p);
  }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("n", ns);
  table.add_column("mean, simulated", mean_sim);
  table.add_column("mean, k/(n+1)", mean_th);
  table.add_column("sd, simulated", sd_sim);
  table.add_column("sd, Beta", sd_th);
  table.add_column("P(coverage < 0.85), simulated", low_sim);
  table.add_column("P(coverage < 0.85), Beta", low_th);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 4;
  md.column_digits = {{"n", 0}};
  std::print("{}\n", table.to_md(md));
  fig.size(1100, 440).fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/cp_beta.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| n | mean, simulated | mean, k/(n+1) | sd, simulated | sd, Beta | P(coverage < 0.85), simulated | P(coverage < 0.85), Beta |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 20 | 0.9042 | 0.9048 | 0.0629 | 0.0626 | 0.1788 | 0.1756 |
| 200 | 0.9007 | 0.9005 | 0.0211 | 0.0211 | 0.0139 | 0.0149 |

\image(figures/cp_beta.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Histograms of the coverage delivered by 20000 calibration sets of size 20 (left) and 200 (right) at \(\alpha=0.1\), with the Beta\((k,n+1-k)\) density (red) and the target 0.9 (black line).)
\alttext(Two histograms with red density curves. Left, n equals 20: a left-skewed histogram from about 0.65 to 1, peaking near 0.95, matched closely by the Beta 19, 2 density, with the 0.9 target marked. Right, n equals 200: a nearly symmetric histogram from 0.84 to 0.96 centred near 0.90, matched by the Beta 181, 20 density.)

The simulated means, standard deviations and tail probabilities agree with the Beta law to
within simulation error. For example, \(P(\text{coverage}<0.85)\) is 0.179 simulated against 0.176 for
\(n=20\), and 0.0139 against 0.0149 for \(n=200\). The practical message is the spread. With 20
calibration points, the average coverage is 90.5%, but about one calibration set in six delivers
less than 85%, and one in 130 less than 70%. With 200, the standard deviation is 0.021. To make the
coverage of *this* calibration set reliably close to the target, choose \(n\) from the Beta law. For
example, \(n=1000\) gives a standard deviation of about 0.01.

> The Palmer penguins

A naive Bayes classifier of species from bill length and depth (the two-predictor model of the
naive Bayes report, 93% accurate) supplies probabilities. The 342 penguins with both measurements
are split at random into thirds for training, calibration (\(n=114\)) and test, 500 times, at
\(\alpha=0.1\). For \(n=114\), \(k=104\) and the expected marginal coverage is \(104/115=0.904\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cp_penguins.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

using DF = dm::dstruct::DataFrame;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto all = dm::datasets::penguins();
  std::vector<std::size_t> keep;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < all.nrows(); ++i)
    if (all.optional_double_at("bill_length_mm", i) && all.optional_double_at("bill_depth_mm", i)) keep.push_back(i);
  const DF data = all.take_rows(keep);
  const std::string formula = "species ~ bill_length_mm + bill_depth_mm";
  const std::size_t n = data.nrows(), third = n / 3;
  std::print("Rows: {}; calibration size {}; expected coverage {:.4f}\n\n", n, third, dm::stats::conformal_coverage(third, 0.1).mean);

  struct Variant {
    std::string name;
    dm::stats::ConformalClassifierOptions options;
  };
  const std::vector<Variant> variants{{"LAC", {.alpha = 0.1, .score = "lac"}},
                                      {"LAC, non-empty", {.alpha = 0.1, .score = "lac", .nonempty = true}},
                                      {"APS", {.alpha = 0.1, .score = "aps"}},
                                      {"APS, non-empty", {.alpha = 0.1, .score = "aps", .nonempty = true}},
                                      {"LAC, class-conditional", {.alpha = 0.1, .score = "lac", .class_conditional = true}}};
  const std::size_t V = variants.size();
  std::vector<double> coverage(V, 0.0), size(V, 0.0), empty(V, 0.0), worst(V, 1.0);
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> class_cov(V, std::vector<double>(3, 0.0)), size_share(V, std::vector<double>(4, 0.0));
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> coverage_draws(V);
  double accuracy = 0.0;
  dm::random::Xoshiro256StarStar rng(21);
  const int reps = 500;
  std::vector<std::string> classes;
  for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
    std::vector<std::size_t> idx(n);
    std::iota(idx.begin(), idx.end(), 0);
    for (std::size_t i = n - 1; i > 0; --i) std::swap(idx[i], idx[std::size_t(rng.uniform(0.0, 1.0) * double(i + 1))]);
    const DF train = data.take_rows({idx.begin(), idx.begin() + long(third)});
    const DF cal = data.take_rows({idx.begin() + long(third), idx.begin() + long(2 * third)});
    const DF test = data.take_rows({idx.begin() + long(2 * third), idx.end()});
    const dm::stats::NaiveBayesClassifier nb(train, formula);
    classes = nb.classes();
    const auto pc = nb.predict_detail(cal), pt = nb.predict_detail(test);
    const auto yc = cal.get_column<std::string>("species").values(), yt = test.get_column<std::string>("species").values();
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < yt.size(); ++i) accuracy += (pt.class_label[i] == yt[i]) / double(yt.size() * reps);
    for (std::size_t v = 0; v < V; ++v) {
      const dm::stats::SplitConformalClassifier cp(pc.probability, yc, classes, variants[v].options);
      const auto ev = cp.evaluate(pt.probability, yt);
      coverage[v] += ev.coverage / reps, size[v] += ev.average_set_size / reps, empty[v] += ev.empty_rate / reps;
      coverage_draws[v].push_back(ev.coverage);
      for (std::size_t c = 0; c < 3; ++c) class_cov[v][c] += ev.class_coverage[c] / reps;
      for (std::size_t s = 0; s < 4; ++s) size_share[v][s] += ev.size_counts[s] / double(yt.size() * reps);
    }
  }
  std::print("Naive Bayes test accuracy, averaged over splits: {:.4f}\n\n", accuracy);
  std::vector<std::string> names;
  std::vector<double> sd_cov;
  for (std::size_t v = 0; v < V; ++v) {
    names.push_back(variants[v].name);
    double m = coverage[v], s = 0.0;
    for (const double c : coverage_draws[v]) s += (c - m) * (c - m) / (reps - 1.0);
    sd_cov.push_back(std::sqrt(s));
  }
  DF table;
  table.add_column("score", names);
  table.add_column("coverage", coverage);
  table.add_column("SD over splits", sd_cov);
  for (std::size_t c = 0; c < 3; ++c) {
    std::vector<double> col;
    for (std::size_t v = 0; v < V; ++v) col.push_back(class_cov[v][c]);
    table.add_column("coverage, " + classes[c], col);
  }
  table.add_column("mean set size", size);
  for (std::size_t s = 0; s < 4; ++s) {
    std::vector<double> col;
    for (std::size_t v = 0; v < V; ++v) col.push_back(size_share[v][s]);
    table.add_column(std::format("share of size {}", s), col);
  }
  DF::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 3;
  std::print("{}\n\n", table.to_md(md));

  // One split for the figure: which set each point of the plane would get (LAC and APS).
  std::vector<std::size_t> idx(n);
  std::iota(idx.begin(), idx.end(), 0);
  dm::random::Xoshiro256StarStar rng2(3);
  for (std::size_t i = n - 1; i > 0; --i) std::swap(idx[i], idx[std::size_t(rng2.uniform(0.0, 1.0) * double(i + 1))]);
  const DF train = data.take_rows({idx.begin(), idx.begin() + long(third)});
  const DF cal = data.take_rows({idx.begin() + long(third), idx.begin() + long(2 * third)});
  const dm::stats::NaiveBayesClassifier nb(train, formula);
  const auto yc = cal.get_column<std::string>("species").values();
  const auto pc = nb.predict_detail(cal);
  // Region codes: 0-2 singletons, 3 {Adelie, Chinstrap}, 4 {Chinstrap, Gentoo}, 5 {Adelie, Gentoo}, 6 all three, 7 empty.
  const std::vector<dm::RGB> fills{dm::graphics::tint({37, 99, 235}, 0.75), dm::graphics::tint({220, 38, 38}, 0.75),
                                   dm::graphics::tint({22, 163, 74}, 0.75), dm::RGB{196, 181, 253},
                                   dm::RGB{253, 230, 138}, dm::RGB{165, 243, 252}, dm::RGB{203, 213, 225}, dm::RGB{255, 255, 255}};
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  for (const std::string score : {"lac", "aps"}) {
    const dm::stats::SplitConformalClassifier cp(pc.probability, yc, nb.classes(), {.alpha = 0.1, .score = score});
    auto code = [&](double bl, double bd) -> std::size_t {
      DF q;
      q.add_column("bill_length_mm", std::vector<double>{bl});
      q.add_column("bill_depth_mm", std::vector<double>{bd});
      const auto in = cp.membership(nb.predict_detail(q).probability[0]);
      const int sz = in[0] + in[1] + in[2];
      if (sz == 0) return 7;
      if (sz == 3) return 6;
      if (sz == 1) return in[0] ? 0 : in[1] ? 1 : 2;
      return !in[2] ? 3 : !in[0] ? 4 : 5;
    };
    auto plt = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    dm::graphics::add_class_regions(plt, code, {31.0, 61.0, 12.5, 25.0}, 150, fills);
    const dm::RGB dots[3] = {{37, 99, 235}, {220, 38, 38}, {22, 163, 74}};
    for (std::size_t c = 0; c < 3; ++c) {
      std::vector<double> x, y;
      for (std::size_t i = 0; i < data.nrows(); ++i)
        if (data.string_at("species", i) == nb.classes()[c]) x.push_back(data.double_at("bill_length_mm", i)), y.push_back(data.double_at("bill_depth_mm", i));
      plt.points(x, y, nb.classes()[c], dots[c], 3.0);
    }
    plt.y_limits(12.5, 25.0).title(score == "lac" ? std::format("LAC sets (threshold {:.5f})", cp.quantile())
                                                  : std::format("APS sets (threshold {:.5f})", cp.quantile()))
        .x_label("bill length (mm)").y_label("bill depth (mm)");
    fig.add(plt);
  }
  fig.size(1100, 500).fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/cp_penguins.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Rows: 342; calibration size 114; expected coverage 0.9043

Naive Bayes test accuracy, averaged over splits: 0.9344

| score | coverage | SD over splits | coverage, Adelie | coverage, Chinstrap | coverage, Gentoo | mean set size | share of size 0 | share of size 1 | share of size 2 | share of size 3 |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| LAC | 0.905 | 0.038 | 0.955 | 0.774 | 0.919 | 0.946 | 0.056 | 0.942 | 0.002 | 0 |
| LAC, non-empty | 0.935 | 0.021 | 0.974 | 0.833 | 0.945 | 1.002 | 0.000 | 0.998 | 0.002 | 0 |
| APS | 0.906 | 0.039 | 0.936 | 0.816 | 0.918 | 1.390 | 0.061 | 0.488 | 0.451 | 0 |
| APS, non-empty | 0.967 | 0.015 | 0.984 | 0.947 | 0.956 | 1.451 | 0.000 | 0.549 | 0.451 | 0 |
| LAC, class-conditional | 0.912 | 0.036 | 0.911 | 0.916 | 0.911 | 1.011 | 0.028 | 0.934 | 0.039 | 0 |


\image(figures/cp_penguins.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The 90% prediction set naive Bayes plus split conformal calibration would give at each point of the bill plane, for one calibration split, with LAC (left) and APS (right) scores. Pale blue, red and green: the singletons Adélie, Chinstrap and Gentoo. Lilac: {Adélie, Chinstrap}; yellow: {Chinstrap, Gentoo}; cyan: {Adélie, Gentoo}; grey: all three; white: empty. All 342 penguins are drawn in their species' colours.)
\alttext(Two maps of the bill length and depth plane with the penguins drawn in blue, red and green. Left, LAC: three pale singleton regions, blue on the left, red upper right and green lower right, separated by thin white bands of empty sets along the boundaries. Right, APS: the singleton regions are pushed outwards, a large lilac region of Adelie-or-Chinstrap sets and a yellow region of Chinstrap-or-Gentoo sets fill the middle, a cyan region of Adelie-or-Gentoo sets lies at lower left, and white empty regions appear in the far corners where the classifier is most certain.)

*Marginal coverage.* Naive Bayes alone is 93.4% accurate. Every conformal variant meets the 90%
target on average. Plain LAC covers 90.5% and plain APS 90.6%, matching the predicted \(104/115=0.904\).
The standard deviation over splits, 0.038, also matches the theory. The Beta law for \(n=114\) gives
0.027 from the calibration set, and 114 test points add binomial noise of about 0.028, for a
total of \(\sqrt{0.027^2+0.028^2}=0.039\).

*Set sizes.* LAC returns a single species for 94.2% of penguins and an empty set for 5.6%; two
species are almost never needed. Its threshold, 0.295 in the figure's split, admits a species
whose probability is at least 0.705. The white bands in the left panel are the empty sets, along
the boundaries where no species is that probable. APS sets are larger (1.39 species on average,
45% of them pairs), because APS admits classes until their total probability reaches a threshold
that is almost 1 here (0.99993 in the figure's split). APS's empty sets (6.1%) are at the
other extreme, in the far corners of the right panel, where the top probability alone exceeds
that threshold. Filling empty sets with the most probable species (`nonempty`) removes them and
raises coverage to 93.5% (LAC) and 96.7% (APS), at little cost in size for LAC.

*Class-conditional coverage.* The marginal guarantee hides an imbalance. Chinstraps, the
smallest species and the one confused with Adélies, are covered only 77.4% of the time under
LAC, while Adélies are covered 95.5%. Class-conditional calibration computes one threshold per
species from about 23 to 50 calibration penguins each. It brings every species to 91.1–91.6%, at
a cost of slightly larger sets (1.011 on average) and some two-species sets (3.9%).

> Conclusion

Split conformal prediction turns any model's output into sets or intervals with a coverage
guarantee that needs only exchangeability. The guarantee is exact: coverage is \(k/(n+1)\), between
\(1-\alpha\) and \(1-\alpha+1/(n+1)\), and the simulations reproduced it to within simulation error at every
calibration size. It costs one held-out calibration set and a sort.

The guarantee is marginal, and that is its main limitation. Coverage averaged over all cases
can hide poor coverage for some of them. The constant-width interval covered 72% where the noise
was large, and LAC covered 77% of Chinstrap penguins. The coverage one calibration set delivers
also varies, as Beta\((k,n+1-k)\); with \(n=20\), one set in six covers less than 85%. The remedies are
in the choice of score and calibration, not in the guarantee. A score that adapts to the
difficulty of each case, such as conformalized quantile regression or normalised residuals for
regression, spreads coverage evenly across \(x\). Class-conditional calibration guarantees each
class separately. A larger calibration set tightens the Beta law. With these choices, conformal
prediction gives practical uncertainty statements for models that have none of their own.

